<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dingzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dingzhou is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. As Zhongshan it was an important city from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and served as the capital of the Later Yan dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> in the late 4th century. It is known for its Liaodi Pagoda, completed in 1055 and one of the tallest brick pagodas in China, and its tomb finds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dingzhou.jpg" alt="Dingzhou" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N509FZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuozhou-Huanghua_Railway_in_Dingzhou_(20170607152759).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
